Hello all,

At the Golf Show Integrity had what they said was a new PE ball valve.  The=
 valve is depicted on the first page of the first attachment, picture immed=
iately below.  Compare to the previous brochure, second attachment, second =
picture below.  Notice the difference in shapes.

[cid:image002.png@01CF3D31.3EEFD2B0]


[cid:image004.png@01CF3D31.3EEFD2B0]


I called Lyall the importer of the Polytec valve made in Korea.  Lyall priv=
ate labels to Integrity and to CMF.  They told me that this new valve is a =
prototype by Polytec but they have not decided to commercialize yet because=
 of the price it is said they have to hit.  It doesn't look like it will be=
 this year.  He called it a "planetary" valve.  Meaning slow to close and s=
low to open.  He mentioned specifically golf courses and that apparently th=
ere was a need.  He seemed unconvinced about the utility of the slow close =
capability.  He said this would be a gas valve and otherwise made of the sa=
me materials as their current valve and it would seem to be offered in addi=
tion to the current valve.  He said they can be used for water but not for =
potable water because of the materials used.  He didn't have anything to sa=
y about the ball lube suitability of water.

He went on to allow they have problems with these valves from time to time =
because "after all it is made in Korea".

He wasn't surprised that Integrity and CMF are representing suitability for=
 potable water and that Integrity is claiming the materials are approved fo=
r water contact as defined by NSF.  He acknowledged this was not correct an=
d the valves should not be used for potable water.


